.TH CLRU_GET 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clru_get \- get a value by key (a type-inferred convenience wrapper)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>

ccol_retval_t clru_get(name, key, val_ptr);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A type-inferred GNU C statement expression that converts key to the
declared KeyT of the cache, in the same way as a plain C assignment,
gets the stored value, and delivers it through val_ptr. The behaviour
on success depends on the value type that you declare at the
construction of the cache:

A val type that is not char*: the macro converts the stored value to
the type of *val_ptr in the same way as a plain C assignment. For
example, a cache that stores int values and reads into a double *
out-parameter converts the int to a double instead of copying the raw
bytes of the int. There is no allocation on the heap. val_ptr is a
pointer to the type of *val_ptr, NOT a cmap_pair *. A type of
*val_ptr that the compiler cannot convert implicitly from the declared
ValT of the cache is a compile error at this point, never a value that
the macro reinterprets in silence.

A char* val type: the macro gives the ownership of the string on the
heap directly to the caller, through *(char **)val_ptr. The caller must
free that pointer, with the custom free function of the cache if the
caller configured a custom allocator at construction, and with free()
if not.

Call clrucache_get_full() directly only when you need the raw cmap_pair
output, which clru_get() hides completely.

A cache with a character-pointer key type accepts a const char * key
without a cast, because the macro keeps the pointer type of such a
key.

A key of a struct type is the same key as another one only when every
byte of the two is equal, padding included; see KEYS WITH PADDING in
clrucache_create_full(3). With GCC 11 or later this macro sets the
padding of its own copy of the key to zero, so a key with equal members
finds the entry that clru_set() stored.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name     Cache variable. The companion type variables, which come from
         clru_construct, clru_declare or clru_redeclare, must be in
         scope. When a cache crosses a function boundary, call
         clru_redeclare() with a simple local alias first, because
         the conversion of the key type and the value type needs the
         companion variables name##__clru_key_type_var and
         name##__clru_val_type_var.
key      Key expression: any expression that the compiler can convert
         implicitly to KeyT.
val_ptr  A pointer to the value type, NOT a cmap_pair *.
         For a val type that is not char*, it gets the stored value,
         converted to the type of *val_ptr, for example a double *
         for a double out-parameter. There is no allocation on the
         heap. The macro leaves *val_ptr untouched when the call does
         not return ccol_success.
         For a char* val type, it is a pointer to a char* variable. On
         success the macro sets it to the string on the heap, and the
         caller must free that string with the matching allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success           The macro delivered the value through val_ptr.
ccol_key_not_found     The key is absent and there is no getter, or
                       the getter failed.
ccol_invalid_args      cache is CLRU_CACHE_INVALID, or cache is a
                       stale handle that a call already destroyed, or
                       key is a NULL character pointer, which is not a
                       string.
ccol_not_enough_memory An internal allocation failed.
ccol_unexpected_failure
                       A remote getter fetched a value whose size does
                       not match the size of ValT. This applies to a
                       val type that is not char*, where sizeof(ValT)
                       is the exact size that the macro needs, and the
                       result is the same whether this call ran the
                       fetch itself or coalesced onto the fetch of
                       another concurrent caller for the same key. The
                       macro leaves val_ptr untouched.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This macro uses a GNU C statement expression ({...}), so it needs GCC
or Clang.
The cache never gives out a pointer into its internal storage; it
always copies the value out before it unlocks the lock of the segment.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
For a char* val type, the string that the macro puts in
*(char **)val_ptr is a heap copy that the caller then owns and MUST
free, with the custom free function of the cache if the caller
configured a custom allocator, and with free() if not; otherwise the
memory leaks. Each call to clru_get for a char* value makes its own
allocation, so two calls for the same key give two independent
pointers, and the caller must free each one.

For a val type that is not char*, there is no allocation on the heap:
the macro converts the value into *val_ptr, and the caller must NOT
call free() on it.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* A value type that is not a string: val_ptr is a double *, not a
   cmap_pair *, and the macro copies the value straight into *val_ptr
   without any allocation on the heap */
clru_construct(cache, int, double, 128, NULL, NULL, NULL);

int k = 5;
double v = 2.71;
clru_set(cache, k, v);

double out = 0.0;
if (clru_get(cache, k, &out) == ccol_success) {
    printf("%.2f\en", out);
}

clru_destroy(cache);

/* A string value type. The caller must free the string it gets */
clru_construct(str_cache, int, char *, 64, NULL, NULL, NULL);

char *greeting = "hello";
clru_set(str_cache, 1, greeting);

char *result = NULL;
if (clru_get(str_cache, 1, &result) == ccol_success) {
    printf("%s\en", result);
    free(result);
}

clru_destroy(str_cache);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clru_set (3),
.BR clrucache_get_full (3)
